# Referrer policy, named by DPIA risk R5.
#
# `no-referrer` rather than the browser default `strict-origin-when-cross-origin`,
# which still sends our origin. Kairos URLs carry member and branch UUIDs in
# their paths (/members/{id}, /departments/{id}), so a referrer sent anywhere
# off-origin discloses that a particular record was being viewed. Nothing here
# depends on referrers — there is no third-party analytics in this application,
# which the privacy notice states — so the strictest value costs nothing.
#
# Being precise about what this does and does not do, because R5 lists the
# missing header as *compounding* the image problem rather than causing it:
# this stops OUR pages leaking OUR urls. It cannot govern what imagedelivery.net
# sends when an image URL is opened directly in a tab, because that response is
# not ours to set headers on. Only authenticated delivery and deletion address
# that, and they are tracked separately on R5.

# Content Security Policy, report-only for now.
#
# WHAT THIS ACTUALLY BUYS, because it is easy to oversell. The session lives
# in localStorage (auth-store.ts), so any cross-site scripting flaw is a
# complete session theft. The obvious answer is a CSP that forbids injected
# script, and this one cannot do that: Next's App Router emits inline
# bootstrap script, and a static Pages deployment has nowhere to mint a
# per-request nonce, so script-src has to allow 'unsafe-inline' - which is
# exactly what an injected inline script needs. Anyone reading this header as
# "XSS is handled" has misread it.
#
# What it does buy is real and worth having:
#   - connect-src pins where the page may send data. An injected script can
#     still read the token out of localStorage; it cannot POST it to the
#     attacker's server. For a token-theft risk, controlling exfiltration is
#     most of the value.
#   - script-src 'self' blocks loading an external script, so the common
#     "inject one tag pointing at evil.example" shape fails.
#   - frame-ancestors 'none' stops the app being framed, which is clickjacking
#     cover that nothing else here provides.
#   - object-src 'none' and base-uri 'self' close two old injection routes
#     that cost nothing to shut.
#
# REPORT-ONLY, deliberately, and this is a temporary state. A policy that
# breaks the fellowship map or profile photographs on a Sunday is worse than
# no policy, because it gets switched off and not switched back on. There is
# no report endpoint, so violations land in the browser console only, which is
# fine while the only traffic is us: click through the app on staging with the
# console open, confirm it is silent, then rename this header to
# Content-Security-Policy and delete this paragraph.
#
# The origins, and why each is here:
#   imagedelivery.net          profile photographs (media/service.ts)
#   nominatim.openstreetmap.org postcode lookup in the fellowship map
#   kairos / staging.kairos     the API, both environments, so one file serves
#                               both Pages deployments
# Fonts are self-hosted by next/font/google at build time, so no Google origin
# is needed. If one appears in the console, something changed.

/*
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://imagedelivery.net; font-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; connect-src 'self' https://kairos.kharis.org https://staging.kairos.kharis.org https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'

# Apple + Android universal-link / app-link association files.
#
# Apple's spec requires application/json + no auth prompt + no redirect. The
# file has no extension, so Cloudflare Pages guesses application/octet-stream —
# force the right MIME here.

/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
  Content-Type: application/json

/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
